\DOC WHILE_TAC

\TYPE {WHILE_TAC : bool ref -> tactic -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Repeatedly applies a tactic to the goal while a supplied boolean reference
remains set, yielding a single transformed goal.

\DESCRIBE
{flag : bool ref} is a mutable flag consulted between iterations: applications
continue while it holds, and the loop stops once the argument tactic clears it.
{tac : tactic} is the tactic run on each iteration; here it advances the proof
state and rewrites the assumptions. The conclusion is left structurally
unchanged except for the final argument of the top-level predicate, which is
replaced by a new term introduced by the iterated tactic. The assumptions are
rewritten: those mentioning the original final argument are discharged and
replaced by corresponding assumptions about the new term, further derived facts
about it are added, and a relational assumption linking the original and new
terms, plus one further derived property, are introduced; assumptions not
mentioning that argument are left in place (sometimes removed and re-added
unchanged).
{
   A, B(t) ?- P t
   ==============================  WHILE_TAC flag tac
   A, B'(u), R t u, C u ?- P u
}
\noindent where the set of added facts {C u} may be empty.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single-goal transformation occurs, all on
goals of the same shape — a stepwise state-advancing predicate with a block of
side conditions as assumptions.

\ENDDOC
